WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:01.840
- Thanks to Express VPN

00:00:01.840 --> 00:00:04.590
for keeping Legal Eagle
safe and in the air.

00:00:04.590 --> 00:00:07.664
In the wake of systemic police abuse

00:00:07.664 --> 00:00:09.760
that was brought to a head by
what we saw with George Floyd,

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:11.992
people are out in the streets

00:00:11.992 --> 00:00:14.076
and they are demanding police reform.

00:00:14.076 --> 00:00:15.830
And it's an idea whose time has come.

00:00:15.830 --> 00:00:18.960
But apart from just entirely
disbanding the police,

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:20.470
what can we possibly do?

00:00:20.470 --> 00:00:22.120
Well, as an officer of the court,

00:00:23.063 --> 00:00:24.340
I have some ideas.

00:00:24.340 --> 00:00:27.670
(dramatic music)

00:00:27.670 --> 00:00:29.930
Hey, legal eagles, it's
time to think like a lawyer,

00:00:29.930 --> 00:00:32.420
because it appears that the time has come

00:00:32.420 --> 00:00:34.370
or true police reform.

00:00:34.370 --> 00:00:37.270
- We cannot settle for anything less

00:00:37.270 --> 00:00:40.243
than transformative structural change.

00:00:41.376 --> 00:00:42.209
- A broad coalition of people

00:00:42.209 --> 00:00:43.920
are working to change the way

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:45.630
that police departments operate.

00:00:45.630 --> 00:00:47.710
Legislators are writing bills.

00:00:47.710 --> 00:00:49.210
State and local governments,

00:00:49.210 --> 00:00:51.151
as well as the federal government

00:00:51.151 --> 00:00:52.597
seem to have heard those cries

00:00:52.597 --> 00:00:54.824
and appear to be amenable for something

00:00:54.824 --> 00:00:55.690
that was completely off the table

00:00:55.690 --> 00:00:57.160
just a few short months ago.

00:00:57.160 --> 00:00:58.340
But that raises the question,

00:00:58.340 --> 00:01:00.880
if we're going to change
the way the police operate,

00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:03.040
what exactly are we going to do?

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:04.550
The good and bad news is that

00:01:04.550 --> 00:01:06.790
a lot of the reforms that
people are calling for

00:01:06.790 --> 00:01:08.890
are very easy to implement.

00:01:08.890 --> 00:01:12.003
It only take the political will to do so.

00:01:12.003 --> 00:01:14.070
And changing the way the police operate

00:01:14.070 --> 00:01:17.291
can be a very, very difficult task

00:01:17.291 --> 00:01:18.732
in a political economy.

00:01:18.732 --> 00:01:21.961
And traditionally speaking,
politicians love the police.

00:01:21.961 --> 00:01:23.320
It's hard to lose an election battle

00:01:23.320 --> 00:01:25.570
if you are on the side
of the police department.

00:01:25.570 --> 00:01:27.827
- We have to respect our police,

00:01:27.827 --> 00:01:29.000
we have to take care of our police.

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:30.120
They're protecting us.

00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:31.246
- And being tough on crime.

00:01:31.246 --> 00:01:32.366
Which is one of the reasons why

00:01:32.366 --> 00:01:33.770
it's been so difficult to
usher in police reform.

00:01:33.770 --> 00:01:37.016
But perhaps the time is now.

00:01:37.016 --> 00:01:39.100
There seems to be a
broad coalition growing

00:01:39.100 --> 00:01:41.040
and calling for real concrete steps.

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:43.387
And the good news is there's tons of

00:01:43.387 --> 00:01:44.951
low hanging fruit for police reform

00:01:44.951 --> 00:01:47.041
that will lead to more just outcomes

00:01:47.041 --> 00:01:48.800
for almost everyone,
and more just policing.

00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:50.820
But before we dive into
some of these reforms,

00:01:50.820 --> 00:01:53.040
some of which I think
are complete no-brainers,

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:54.760
let's talk for a second about the problems

00:01:54.760 --> 00:01:56.410
with maximalism and trade-offs.

00:01:56.410 --> 00:01:59.033
Because in this debate
people want different things.

00:01:59.913 --> 00:02:01.240
Some people want safety, low crime.

00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:04.049
Others want human dignity and justice.

00:02:04.049 --> 00:02:05.950
Tax payers want low cost and low taxes.

00:02:05.950 --> 00:02:07.360
Some people want higher enforcement

00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:09.657
of crimes and punishment rates.

00:02:09.657 --> 00:02:11.160
Police officers often want job safety,

00:02:11.160 --> 00:02:13.420
and other individuals want privacy.

00:02:13.420 --> 00:02:16.044
And a lot of these different things

00:02:16.044 --> 00:02:16.877
are at odds with each other.

00:02:16.877 --> 00:02:19.103
And we have to recognize that,

00:02:19.103 --> 00:02:20.878
when you turn one of these dials,

00:02:20.878 --> 00:02:21.711
the other dials move.

00:02:21.711 --> 00:02:23.810
So, it's possible that
some of these reforms

00:02:23.810 --> 00:02:25.650
might cost more tax money.

00:02:25.650 --> 00:02:28.360
Some of these reforms might result in

00:02:28.360 --> 00:02:30.590
fewer crimes being punished

00:02:30.590 --> 00:02:32.803
when the crime actually occurs.

00:02:33.863 --> 00:02:35.309
Some of these reforms might cause

00:02:35.309 --> 00:02:37.761
police officers to be
less safe in their jobs.

00:02:37.761 --> 00:02:39.380
And these are all trade-offs.

00:02:39.380 --> 00:02:41.270
I'm not here to choose which one

00:02:41.270 --> 00:02:43.519
is more important than the other.

00:02:43.519 --> 00:02:46.530
But all of these dials move in
conjunction with each other,

00:02:46.530 --> 00:02:47.450
and there are going to be trade-offs

00:02:47.450 --> 00:02:50.384
with some of these particular reforms.

00:02:50.384 --> 00:02:51.920
I happen to personally believe that

00:02:51.920 --> 00:02:54.780
we should move the dial in terms of

00:02:54.780 --> 00:02:58.720
justice and fairness with
respect to individual citizens.

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:01.124
And if that means some higher costs,

00:03:01.124 --> 00:03:03.170
well, that might be a
trade-off that's worth while.

00:03:03.170 --> 00:03:06.223
But it would be very silly to claim that

00:03:06.223 --> 00:03:08.821
all of these reforms are
going to be a free lunch.

00:03:08.821 --> 00:03:10.070
There's no such thing as a free lunch.

00:03:10.070 --> 00:03:12.330
But let's talk about some of the reforms

00:03:12.330 --> 00:03:14.104
that are in the air.

00:03:14.104 --> 00:03:15.030
I'm not here to advocate for any

00:03:15.030 --> 00:03:16.838
particular one of these,

00:03:16.838 --> 00:03:18.040
but I think a lot of
these potential reforms

00:03:18.040 --> 00:03:21.644
have the potential to
really be a game-changer

00:03:21.644 --> 00:03:24.205
when it comes to justice and fairness

00:03:24.205 --> 00:03:25.975
throughout America when
it comes to the police.

00:03:25.975 --> 00:03:27.420
So, in no particular order,

00:03:27.420 --> 00:03:28.500
here are some ideas that really

00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:30.250
could make a huge difference when

00:03:31.144 --> 00:03:32.030
it comes to fairness and the police.

00:03:32.030 --> 00:03:33.930
First, body cameras.

00:03:33.930 --> 00:03:36.640
A lot of jurisdictions
don't have body cameras.

00:03:36.640 --> 00:03:38.700
Luckily there are a
lot of federal programs

00:03:38.700 --> 00:03:40.430
that help provide funding to

00:03:40.430 --> 00:03:41.940
local municipal police departments

00:03:41.940 --> 00:03:44.140
so that they can provide body cameras

00:03:44.140 --> 00:03:46.000
when it wouldn't otherwise
be in the budget.

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:48.200
But the technology exists for body cameras

00:03:48.200 --> 00:03:50.210
on police officers to record basically

00:03:50.210 --> 00:03:52.820
continuously while that
police officer is patrolling.

00:03:52.820 --> 00:03:54.860
And it seems crazy that the police officer

00:03:54.860 --> 00:03:57.240
would have the control to be able to

00:03:57.240 --> 00:04:01.089
turn off and on the body camera at whim.

00:04:01.089 --> 00:04:02.300
And we've seen instances where

00:04:02.300 --> 00:04:06.410
police officers have actually
planted drugs on suspects

00:04:06.410 --> 00:04:10.226
and then turn their body
camera on minutes later.

00:04:10.226 --> 00:04:11.509
And the only reason we know that is

00:04:11.509 --> 00:04:13.274
that the police officer didn't know

00:04:13.274 --> 00:04:15.520
that the body camera had
a prerecord functionality.

00:04:15.520 --> 00:04:16.960
But let's do away with all of that

00:04:16.960 --> 00:04:18.640
and just have body
cameras on all the time.

00:04:18.640 --> 00:04:21.447
And there could be penalties
for police officers

00:04:21.447 --> 00:04:22.280
that intentionally block or turn off

00:04:22.280 --> 00:04:24.490
their body cameras
during important events.

00:04:24.490 --> 00:04:26.070
And this is one reform that I think

00:04:26.070 --> 00:04:28.330
most police officers
should be in favor of.

00:04:28.330 --> 00:04:31.239
Because, while the cultural zeitgeist

00:04:31.239 --> 00:04:34.769
is full of horrific accounts
of police misconduct,

00:04:34.769 --> 00:04:35.920
especially in the wake
of peaceful protests,

00:04:35.920 --> 00:04:37.980
it'd be silly to think that all of the

00:04:37.980 --> 00:04:41.666
complaints against the
police are meritorious.

00:04:41.666 --> 00:04:43.100
In fact, probably, the
majority of the complaints

00:04:43.100 --> 00:04:45.820
that are made against the
police are not meritorious

00:04:45.820 --> 00:04:48.405
and are probably made by criminals

00:04:48.405 --> 00:04:50.340
who are not happy with the fact

00:04:50.340 --> 00:04:52.254
that they were arrested.

00:04:52.254 --> 00:04:53.530
So, having footage of interactions

00:04:53.530 --> 00:04:55.200
with potential suspects where the police

00:04:55.200 --> 00:04:58.823
can clearly show that they
engaged in no misconduct

00:04:58.823 --> 00:05:00.660
inures to the benefits
of the police as well.

00:05:00.660 --> 00:05:02.670
And next is the issue of public access

00:05:02.670 --> 00:05:05.010
to information regarding
police misconduct.

00:05:05.010 --> 00:05:06.980
Now, we've already acknowledged that

00:05:07.966 --> 00:05:09.260
some police misconduct or allegations

00:05:09.260 --> 00:05:11.290
of police misconduct is
completely fabricated.

00:05:11.290 --> 00:05:14.068
But also, some of that information

00:05:14.068 --> 00:05:15.833
is absolute not fabricated,

00:05:15.833 --> 00:05:18.770
and we have to admit that the
police engage in misconduct.

00:05:18.770 --> 00:05:22.090
And it's important that because
police are public servants

00:05:22.090 --> 00:05:25.530
that not just convictions
regarding police misconduct,

00:05:25.530 --> 00:05:27.770
but also even allegations
of police misconduct

00:05:27.770 --> 00:05:30.380
be made available to both the cities

00:05:30.380 --> 00:05:33.373
and the people who those cities serve.

00:05:34.552 --> 00:05:37.154
And the statistics tend
to bear this out as well.

00:05:37.154 --> 00:05:38.690
Only a small number of police officers

00:05:38.690 --> 00:05:41.310
are responsible for the vast majority

00:05:41.310 --> 00:05:44.550
of allegations and convictions
of police misconduct.

00:05:44.550 --> 00:05:46.789
So, it's very important to have

00:05:46.789 --> 00:05:48.220
access to that data, that information

00:05:48.220 --> 00:05:50.870
to know who those
potential bad apples are.

00:05:50.870 --> 00:05:54.806
- You always have a bad
apple no matter where you go.

00:05:54.806 --> 00:05:57.487
You have bad apples, and there
are not too many of them.

00:05:57.487 --> 00:05:58.320
And I can tell you, there are not too

00:05:58.320 --> 00:06:00.133
many of them in the police department.

00:06:00.133 --> 00:06:01.900
- And by the way, the
metaphor of the bad apples

00:06:01.900 --> 00:06:04.145
that's being thrown around a lot,

00:06:04.145 --> 00:06:05.560
especially by police officers
and police departments

00:06:05.560 --> 00:06:07.410
claiming that it's only
a couple of bad apples.

00:06:07.410 --> 00:06:10.300
Well, remember, that analogy is that

00:06:10.300 --> 00:06:13.090
a handful of bad apples will spoil

00:06:13.090 --> 00:06:15.522
the entire barrel of of apples.

00:06:15.522 --> 00:06:18.200
So, when you have a
potential systemic issue,

00:06:18.200 --> 00:06:20.335
the fact that you're pointing to

00:06:20.335 --> 00:06:21.670
a couple of bad apples doesn't actually

00:06:21.670 --> 00:06:24.590
support your argument
that it's individuals

00:06:24.590 --> 00:06:25.850
and not the system itself.

00:06:25.850 --> 00:06:28.990
Because those couple of bad actors,

00:06:28.990 --> 00:06:30.200
those bad apples can have
a corrupting influence

00:06:30.200 --> 00:06:31.830
on the rest of the entire department.

00:06:31.830 --> 00:06:34.220
So, be careful what metaphors you use

00:06:34.220 --> 00:06:36.368
and in what particular context.

00:06:36.368 --> 00:06:37.890
It's important to know
what those words mean.

00:06:37.890 --> 00:06:41.337
But even if we were to take the idea

00:06:41.337 --> 00:06:42.770
that these are isolated individuals

00:06:42.770 --> 00:06:45.110
that don't have a larger systemic issue,

00:06:45.110 --> 00:06:47.420
one of the more meta things
that police departments

00:06:47.420 --> 00:06:49.290
I think should do is inculcate

00:06:49.290 --> 00:06:51.400
this culture of weeding out the bad apples

00:06:51.400 --> 00:06:53.527
from the rest of the department.

00:06:53.527 --> 00:06:55.449
Certainly, there are
going to be individuals

00:06:55.449 --> 00:06:56.640
who are worse than other individuals,

00:06:56.640 --> 00:06:58.460
and they might have a
corrupting influence.

00:06:58.460 --> 00:07:00.850
And the stats bear out that they might

00:07:00.850 --> 00:07:04.267
be responsible for more of the
misconduct than other people.

00:07:04.267 --> 00:07:05.400
So, if you are a good police officer

00:07:05.400 --> 00:07:06.940
who is not engaging in misconduct,

00:07:06.940 --> 00:07:10.191
you should want to get
those other people out.

00:07:10.191 --> 00:07:11.320
But what we've seen is that this blue wall

00:07:11.320 --> 00:07:14.800
or this blue line prevents
getting out the bad actors.

00:07:14.800 --> 00:07:16.940
So, I would like to see, and I don't know

00:07:17.935 --> 00:07:18.768
how one would accomplish this per se,

00:07:18.768 --> 00:07:20.900
but police departments
should have a culture

00:07:20.900 --> 00:07:22.720
of weeding out the bad apples,

00:07:22.720 --> 00:07:24.440
because they shouldn't be there.

00:07:24.440 --> 00:07:26.570
One way you might do that is by having

00:07:26.570 --> 00:07:29.437
a whistle blower system that tends to

00:07:29.437 --> 00:07:31.045
exist on the federal level.

00:07:31.045 --> 00:07:32.830
Though that's being eroded day-by-day.

00:07:32.830 --> 00:07:35.010
Still, the idea of whistle blowers

00:07:35.010 --> 00:07:37.410
or providing rewards to individuals

00:07:37.410 --> 00:07:39.260
who are willing to buck the trend

00:07:39.260 --> 00:07:41.150
of the rest of the department

00:07:41.150 --> 00:07:43.940
to be able to report and get rid of

00:07:43.940 --> 00:07:45.610
bad actors where they exist.

00:07:45.610 --> 00:07:49.090
I think that that is a
very meritorious system,

00:07:49.090 --> 00:07:51.080
a good philosophy, and I don't know

00:07:51.080 --> 00:07:53.540
how one would do that, but it's something

00:07:53.540 --> 00:07:56.390
that police departments should aspire to.

00:07:56.390 --> 00:07:58.468
Though, on the other side,
if that is the carrot

00:07:58.468 --> 00:08:00.040
in terms of getting people
to report bad behavior,

00:08:00.040 --> 00:08:03.060
the stick portion of that
would be a duty to report,

00:08:03.060 --> 00:08:07.291
such that if you witness
bad conduct taking place,

00:08:07.291 --> 00:08:08.600
and this does exist in
some police departments,

00:08:08.600 --> 00:08:10.500
then the individual officers have

00:08:10.500 --> 00:08:13.360
a duty to report that
bad conduct up the chain

00:08:13.360 --> 00:08:15.230
or to investigative affairs.

00:08:15.230 --> 00:08:17.874
But you could take a two-pronged approach.

00:08:17.874 --> 00:08:21.078
You can reward whistle
blowers who come forward,

00:08:21.078 --> 00:08:22.160
and you can also create a duty to report

00:08:22.160 --> 00:08:24.440
such that individual officers are liable

00:08:24.440 --> 00:08:27.360
when they fail to report
egregious conduct.

00:08:27.360 --> 00:08:28.810
But when you're talking about

00:08:29.652 --> 00:08:31.666
the culture of the police department,

00:08:31.666 --> 00:08:33.440
it's impossible to overlook
the elephant in the room,

00:08:33.440 --> 00:08:35.740
and that's police unions.

00:08:35.740 --> 00:08:37.870
This is probably one potential avenue

00:08:37.870 --> 00:08:39.920
that's going to be the most divisive,

00:08:39.920 --> 00:08:42.620
even amongst people that want
to reform police departments.

00:08:42.620 --> 00:08:45.190
But there is no argument
that police unions

00:08:45.190 --> 00:08:47.320
have a huge amount of power.

00:08:47.320 --> 00:08:49.500
And partly, it's by design.

00:08:49.500 --> 00:08:51.500
Police unions are designed

00:08:51.500 --> 00:08:54.932
so that they create
collective bargaining power

00:08:54.932 --> 00:08:56.780
so that they can help police officers

00:08:56.780 --> 00:08:58.660
get more favorable working conditions

00:08:58.660 --> 00:09:01.230
and more favorable terms
under which they work.

00:09:01.230 --> 00:09:03.830
The downside to that is that

00:09:03.830 --> 00:09:06.250
the police union has as a client

00:09:06.250 --> 00:09:08.000
its police officers.

00:09:08.000 --> 00:09:11.210
- I will defend those that stand here.

00:09:11.210 --> 00:09:13.580
I will defend my family.

00:09:13.580 --> 00:09:17.270
I will defend our citizens, both here

00:09:17.270 --> 00:09:21.350
and who are now in phase
one going back to work.

00:09:21.350 --> 00:09:23.230
- And often, what's in the best interest

00:09:23.230 --> 00:09:25.710
of the community is not
in the best interest

00:09:25.710 --> 00:09:27.550
of the police officers themselves

00:09:27.550 --> 00:09:29.540
or the police union at large.

00:09:29.540 --> 00:09:31.979
And part of the issue here is that

00:09:31.979 --> 00:09:33.210
some of the things that the police unions

00:09:33.210 --> 00:09:36.060
often negotiate over
are not just things like

00:09:36.060 --> 00:09:39.460
salary, or hours, or retirement benefits,

00:09:39.460 --> 00:09:41.900
but things like what happens when

00:09:41.900 --> 00:09:45.180
individual police officers
are accused of misconduct.

00:09:45.180 --> 00:09:47.640
Often, you'll see in the
collective bargaining agreements,

00:09:47.640 --> 00:09:49.200
that the police officers,

00:09:49.200 --> 00:09:51.500
if they are accused of
egregious misconduct,

00:09:51.500 --> 00:09:53.330
have a certain cooling off period,

00:09:53.330 --> 00:09:55.340
where it might be 48 or 72 hours

00:09:55.340 --> 00:09:58.057
before other police officers

00:09:58.057 --> 00:10:00.280
are even allowed to
interview the police officers

00:10:00.280 --> 00:10:03.710
accused of misconduct of
what actually happened.

00:10:03.710 --> 00:10:05.400
And what we have seen is that

00:10:05.400 --> 00:10:07.110
during that amount of time,

00:10:07.110 --> 00:10:09.500
think about what they're going to say

00:10:09.500 --> 00:10:11.360
to the people who are investigating.

00:10:11.360 --> 00:10:14.113
They think about getting
their stories straight.

00:10:15.095 --> 00:10:16.510
They think about what
particular chain of events

00:10:16.510 --> 00:10:18.840
would be most benefitial to themselves.

00:10:18.840 --> 00:10:21.184
And it will not surprise you that

00:10:21.184 --> 00:10:22.350
if you are a normal citizen who is

00:10:22.350 --> 00:10:24.000
accused of criminal misconduct,

00:10:24.000 --> 00:10:26.350
you don't get the same kind of protections

00:10:26.350 --> 00:10:30.002
that the police do
because of the contracts

00:10:30.002 --> 00:10:32.150
that are written into the
collective bargaining agreements

00:10:32.150 --> 00:10:34.440
between the police union
and the police departments.

00:10:34.440 --> 00:10:37.569
And I've heard stories of police unions

00:10:37.569 --> 00:10:39.050
giving individual officers, more or less,

00:10:39.050 --> 00:10:42.510
a script to recite when
they are accused of

00:10:43.470 --> 00:10:44.303
unlawful use of force.

00:10:44.303 --> 00:10:46.940
So, they can get their story to line up

00:10:46.940 --> 00:10:50.523
with the thing that is
the best legal defense.

00:10:50.523 --> 00:10:52.380
- And the only thing
actually left was my firearm.

00:10:52.380 --> 00:10:55.336
This has to work, otherwise
I'm going to be dead.

00:10:55.336 --> 00:10:56.169
He's gonna get this gun away from me.

00:10:56.169 --> 00:10:57.850
Something gonna happen,
and I'm gonna be dead.

00:10:57.850 --> 00:11:00.050
- And these are just a
handful for examples how,

00:11:00.050 --> 00:11:02.700
in many ways, police officers
get special treatment

00:11:02.700 --> 00:11:05.680
when they are accused of
misconduct on their own.

00:11:05.680 --> 00:11:07.100
And that's not to say that police unions

00:11:07.100 --> 00:11:08.840
don't provide real benefits.

00:11:08.840 --> 00:11:11.100
But if people are really serious about

00:11:11.100 --> 00:11:13.450
creating a more just and fair system

00:11:13.450 --> 00:11:15.430
for everyone, not just police officers,

00:11:15.430 --> 00:11:16.770
certainly people are gonna have to take

00:11:16.770 --> 00:11:19.390
a serious look at the
power the police unions

00:11:19.390 --> 00:11:20.650
have in this country.

00:11:20.650 --> 00:11:22.100
And because of the power the

00:11:22.100 --> 00:11:24.270
police unions have in this country,

00:11:24.270 --> 00:11:25.830
it's often been incredibly difficult

00:11:25.830 --> 00:11:28.700
to get police reforms because,
as a political entity,

00:11:28.700 --> 00:11:32.383
they tend to fight any
kind of change at all.

00:11:32.383 --> 00:11:33.730
- Beginning today, as chief,

00:11:33.730 --> 00:11:36.394
I am immediately withdrawing from

00:11:36.394 --> 00:11:37.990
the contract negotiations

00:11:37.990 --> 00:11:39.598
with the Minneapolis Police Federation.

00:11:39.598 --> 00:11:41.210
I plan to bring in
subject matter experience

00:11:41.210 --> 00:11:44.790
and advisors to conduct a thorough review

00:11:44.790 --> 00:11:47.110
of how the contract can be restructured

00:11:47.110 --> 00:11:49.720
to provide greater community transparency

00:11:49.720 --> 00:11:52.270
and more flexibility for true reform.

00:11:52.270 --> 00:11:55.860
- So, if we were going to
use a general principle here,

00:11:55.860 --> 00:11:57.906
we might say that perhaps there can be

00:11:57.906 --> 00:11:59.450
a rule that says police officers

00:11:59.450 --> 00:12:01.130
can't get any special treatment

00:12:01.130 --> 00:12:03.510
above and beyond what
any other normal citizen

00:12:03.510 --> 00:12:06.523
would get when accused
of criminal misconduct.

00:12:06.523 --> 00:12:08.070
And when it comes to police oversight,

00:12:08.070 --> 00:12:10.310
perhaps we should rethink the idea

00:12:10.310 --> 00:12:12.390
that the police are able
to police themselves.

00:12:12.390 --> 00:12:15.280
Where instead of the police
overseeing themselves,

00:12:15.280 --> 00:12:17.200
there is a board of civilians,

00:12:17.200 --> 00:12:19.553
or at least non police officers,

00:12:19.553 --> 00:12:21.530
who are able to look at
the potential misconduct

00:12:21.530 --> 00:12:23.877
and make a judgment over it.

00:12:23.877 --> 00:12:24.760
And perhaps, even better than just

00:12:24.760 --> 00:12:26.600
a civilian oversight board

00:12:26.600 --> 00:12:27.433
is a civilian oversight board with

00:12:27.433 --> 00:12:29.420
the ability to make
high-stakes decisions that,

00:12:29.420 --> 00:12:32.140
if they do find misconduct
they are able to

00:12:32.140 --> 00:12:34.070
levee penalties against
the police officers

00:12:34.070 --> 00:12:36.227
or potentially even fire them.

00:12:36.227 --> 00:12:37.620
Now, obviously, this is something that

00:12:37.620 --> 00:12:38.453
police unions want to fight against,

00:12:38.453 --> 00:12:41.100
because they don't want
some other third party

00:12:41.100 --> 00:12:44.440
having sway over
individual police officers,

00:12:44.440 --> 00:12:47.260
but it's something that other
police departments have done

00:12:47.260 --> 00:12:50.650
and can make sure that,
when there is misconduct,

00:12:50.650 --> 00:12:53.781
that the bad actors or punished for it.

00:12:53.781 --> 00:12:56.446
And on that score, perhaps and obvious one

00:12:56.446 --> 00:12:57.950
is really consequences for bad conduct.

00:12:57.950 --> 00:13:01.420
When there is a conviction
for police misconduct

00:13:01.420 --> 00:13:04.463
there need to be real stakes so that

00:13:04.463 --> 00:13:06.910
individuals are punished for engaging

00:13:06.910 --> 00:13:08.530
in that kind of misconduct.

00:13:08.530 --> 00:13:11.735
In one way that is sort of in the

00:13:11.735 --> 00:13:13.779
cultural zeitgeist these days is

00:13:13.779 --> 00:13:15.900
ending or limiting what's
called qualified immunity.

00:13:15.900 --> 00:13:18.860
Now, this is a topic for another day.

00:13:18.860 --> 00:13:22.050
I'm going to do another set of videos

00:13:22.050 --> 00:13:24.580
regarding what's called
a section 1983 claim

00:13:24.580 --> 00:13:28.200
under federal law,
which is limited by this

00:13:28.200 --> 00:13:31.109
immunity that police
officers often received

00:13:31.109 --> 00:13:32.398
called qualified immunity,

00:13:32.398 --> 00:13:34.150
which is an impediment
to some federal lawsuits

00:13:34.150 --> 00:13:36.900
being served against
individual police officers

00:13:36.900 --> 00:13:38.650
and individual police departments.

00:13:38.650 --> 00:13:43.510
But, suffice to say, there
are bills before congress

00:13:43.510 --> 00:13:45.600
that would abrogate this immunity,

00:13:45.600 --> 00:13:47.370
this qualified immunity for the

00:13:47.370 --> 00:13:49.871
police officers and police departments.

00:13:49.871 --> 00:13:50.970
Which might provide an incentive

00:13:50.970 --> 00:13:53.030
to not engage in misconduct or

00:13:53.030 --> 00:13:54.999
at least try and curtail it,

00:13:54.999 --> 00:13:57.080
because if there are all these lawsuits,

00:13:57.080 --> 00:13:59.220
then the police departments
don't want to get defunded.

00:13:59.220 --> 00:14:02.865
So, I will cover that in
depth in future videos,

00:14:02.865 --> 00:14:05.030
because it's a very,
very complicated subject.

00:14:05.030 --> 00:14:08.020
And in terms of the real nuts and bolts,

00:14:08.020 --> 00:14:12.224
there is a movement on social
media called 8 Can't Wait,

00:14:12.224 --> 00:14:13.350
for eight particular tactics

00:14:13.350 --> 00:14:15.110
that a particular group thinks

00:14:16.045 --> 00:14:18.091
would make a big difference in terms of

00:14:18.091 --> 00:14:19.760
making police departments less violent.

00:14:19.760 --> 00:14:21.720
These eight particular tactics include:

00:14:21.720 --> 00:14:23.070
banning choke holds outright,

00:14:23.070 --> 00:14:24.470
which many police departments have done,

00:14:24.470 --> 00:14:27.848
although Minneapolis is a
notable exception to that;

00:14:27.848 --> 00:14:28.980
requiring deescalation before

00:14:28.980 --> 00:14:31.120
moving forward with violent force;

00:14:31.120 --> 00:14:33.450
requiring a warning before shooting;

00:14:33.450 --> 00:14:36.460
exhausting all of their
means before shooting;

00:14:36.460 --> 00:14:38.480
creating a duty to intervene so that

00:14:39.429 --> 00:14:42.670
when police officers
see criminal misconduct

00:14:42.670 --> 00:14:44.680
or can help an individual,

00:14:44.680 --> 00:14:46.820
they intervene in that situation;

00:14:46.820 --> 00:14:49.860
banning shooting at moving vehicles,

00:14:49.860 --> 00:14:52.745
requiring a use of force
continuum before escalating;

00:14:52.745 --> 00:14:56.879
and requiring comprehensive
reporting of use of force.

00:14:56.879 --> 00:14:58.359
I'd recommend taking a deeper dive

00:14:58.359 --> 00:14:59.804
into the stats that this movement

00:14:59.804 --> 00:15:01.190
has offered about the efficacy.

00:15:01.190 --> 00:15:03.485
But there's definitely some potential

00:15:03.485 --> 00:15:05.093
in some of these reforms to create

00:15:05.093 --> 00:15:06.695
a less violent police department.

00:15:06.695 --> 00:15:08.935
Now, in a prior video, I mentioned that

00:15:08.935 --> 00:15:10.520
saying police officers are soldiers

00:15:10.520 --> 00:15:14.520
in a war for America makes them
sort of feel like soldiers.

00:15:14.520 --> 00:15:16.840
And when that happens the police acts

00:15:16.840 --> 00:15:19.070
more like soldiers than actual police.

00:15:19.070 --> 00:15:20.630
But one of the other reasons why

00:15:20.630 --> 00:15:25.490
police officers engage
in additional military

00:15:25.490 --> 00:15:28.600
or violent tactics is that they are

00:15:29.438 --> 00:15:32.140
clad in all kinds of
surplus military equipment.

00:15:32.140 --> 00:15:34.315
Now, one of the reasons that we've

00:15:34.315 --> 00:15:35.148
seen that over the last few years

00:15:35.148 --> 00:15:36.790
is that there is a federal law

00:15:37.681 --> 00:15:38.514
that allows the federal government

00:15:38.514 --> 00:15:40.860
to give police departments surplus

00:15:40.860 --> 00:15:44.100
military equipment at cost or at no cost,

00:15:44.100 --> 00:15:48.902
so long as they use that
equipment within one year.

00:15:48.902 --> 00:15:50.673
So, I probably don't need to tell you

00:15:50.673 --> 00:15:52.908
that this creates all kinds
of backwards incentives.

00:15:52.908 --> 00:15:54.060
Number one, police departments are

00:15:54.060 --> 00:15:56.281
going to want to do what they can

00:15:56.281 --> 00:15:58.030
to get free military equipment.

00:15:58.030 --> 00:16:00.650
And number two, the law
as currently written

00:16:01.575 --> 00:16:02.770
incentivizes them to actually use it too

00:16:02.770 --> 00:16:04.920
on a use it or lose it basis.

00:16:04.920 --> 00:16:07.020
And I'll be the first to admit

00:16:07.020 --> 00:16:10.067
that driving around in a
repurposed Humvee or MRAP

00:16:10.067 --> 00:16:10.980
looks like a whole lot of fun.

00:16:10.980 --> 00:16:12.960
But the last thing that we want to do

00:16:12.960 --> 00:16:15.640
is to make police feel like they're

00:16:15.640 --> 00:16:17.760
soldiers on the front lines of America.

00:16:17.760 --> 00:16:21.053
That's not how you get
just outcomes for citizens.

00:16:21.932 --> 00:16:24.090
It's no wonder that
police wearing riot gear

00:16:24.090 --> 00:16:26.340
being transported around in troop carriers

00:16:26.340 --> 00:16:28.342
feel like soldiers.

00:16:28.342 --> 00:16:29.510
And that leads to escalation,

00:16:29.510 --> 00:16:31.300
and we've seen that with
peaceful protesters.

00:16:31.300 --> 00:16:34.300
And that seems like the very
last thing that we want.

00:16:34.300 --> 00:16:35.810
And another potential way to limit

00:16:35.810 --> 00:16:37.430
the number of violent encounters

00:16:37.430 --> 00:16:39.060
that happened with police,

00:16:39.060 --> 00:16:41.330
in sort of outside the box thinking is

00:16:41.330 --> 00:16:45.260
perhaps we need to send
non-police sometimes.

00:16:45.260 --> 00:16:48.543
In America, pretty much everyone knows

00:16:48.543 --> 00:16:50.213
how to dial the police, 911.

00:16:51.151 --> 00:16:53.201
But often, the police might not

00:16:53.201 --> 00:16:55.440
be the right people, the
right public servants

00:16:55.440 --> 00:16:57.510
to send to any given situation.

00:16:57.510 --> 00:17:00.310
Perhaps we need to rethink
when we deploy the police.

00:17:00.310 --> 00:17:02.750
So, for example, say that someone

00:17:02.750 --> 00:17:04.300
is having an issue with someone

00:17:05.391 --> 00:17:06.993
who has a mental health issue.

00:17:06.993 --> 00:17:07.826
Instead of sending the police,

00:17:07.826 --> 00:17:10.360
which is basically the default in America,

00:17:10.360 --> 00:17:13.890
perhaps we can send a
non-police health worker,

00:17:13.890 --> 00:17:14.723
or a social worker.

00:17:14.723 --> 00:17:17.290
That if things escalate,
if things are likely

00:17:17.290 --> 00:17:19.497
to get potentially violent,

00:17:19.497 --> 00:17:23.270
or if there is a violent
crime potentially perpetrated,

00:17:23.270 --> 00:17:26.068
then at that point the
police can be called.

00:17:26.068 --> 00:17:27.810
But perhaps the first person who is called

00:17:27.810 --> 00:17:30.399
is not a police officer.

00:17:30.399 --> 00:17:31.688
But that does remind me of something

00:17:31.688 --> 00:17:33.220
that I've seen in
particular in Washington DC,

00:17:33.220 --> 00:17:35.720
which is that when police
officers are called,

00:17:35.720 --> 00:17:38.080
I think everyone would
agree those officers

00:17:38.080 --> 00:17:40.010
need to be clearly identified.

00:17:40.010 --> 00:17:41.960
Recently we've seen all kinds of

00:17:41.960 --> 00:17:44.650
unidentified military and police officers

00:17:44.650 --> 00:17:46.610
roaming the streets of Washington DC

00:17:46.610 --> 00:17:48.130
with no clear identification.

00:17:48.130 --> 00:17:51.082
And I think a very, very good thing

00:17:51.082 --> 00:17:53.329
that pretty much everyone
has a video camera

00:17:53.329 --> 00:17:55.256
in their pocket and they're able to

00:17:55.256 --> 00:17:56.550
document police misconduct
when it happens,

00:17:56.550 --> 00:17:58.936
and we've certainly seen hundreds

00:17:58.936 --> 00:18:02.860
if not thousands of examples
of that during the protests.

00:18:02.860 --> 00:18:06.620
Obviously, some force
was probably justified

00:18:06.620 --> 00:18:07.760
during riots and looting.

00:18:07.760 --> 00:18:10.710
But, by and large, the
protests have been peaceful.

00:18:10.710 --> 00:18:13.030
So, when you have police using force,

00:18:13.030 --> 00:18:15.820
using rubber bullets, using tear gas,

00:18:15.820 --> 00:18:18.136
we need to know who these individuals are.

00:18:18.136 --> 00:18:20.190
Let alone times when they're using force,

00:18:20.190 --> 00:18:23.170
deadly or not, to subdue
potential suspects.

00:18:23.170 --> 00:18:24.940
And in terms of protests,
we might consider

00:18:24.940 --> 00:18:28.623
banning the use of rubber
bullets and tear gas in general.

00:18:28.623 --> 00:18:31.827
It's not clear that the police
have the rules available

00:18:31.827 --> 00:18:33.550
to use that kind of equipment responsibly.

00:18:33.550 --> 00:18:36.020
Now, obviously, there are times when

00:18:36.886 --> 00:18:38.680
there are going to be protests

00:18:38.680 --> 00:18:41.048
and riots that are much more violent

00:18:41.048 --> 00:18:42.280
than what we've seen as of late.

00:18:42.280 --> 00:18:45.010
But perhaps, as a general rule,

00:18:45.010 --> 00:18:48.410
or with a very specific
escalation of force,

00:18:48.410 --> 00:18:50.290
we might put rules in place that

00:18:50.290 --> 00:18:55.290
prevent them from using those
tools in these circumstances.

00:18:55.641 --> 00:18:56.590
And of course, it's
important to acknowledge

00:18:56.590 --> 00:18:59.490
that police have a dangerous job.

00:18:59.490 --> 00:19:01.070
But sometimes that danger is overblown.

00:19:01.070 --> 00:19:03.190
If I can digress for a moment,

00:19:03.190 --> 00:19:04.840
currently the stats show that

00:19:05.742 --> 00:19:07.450
the police officer is the 14th most

00:19:07.450 --> 00:19:10.040
dangerous job in the United States.

00:19:10.040 --> 00:19:14.241
Somewhere between taxi drivers
and construction workers.

00:19:14.241 --> 00:19:15.843
So, sure, being a police officer

00:19:15.843 --> 00:19:16.676
might be considered a dangerous job,

00:19:16.676 --> 00:19:18.820
but there are many, many other jobs

00:19:18.820 --> 00:19:21.456
that are actually much more dangerous

00:19:21.456 --> 00:19:22.739
than being a police officer.

00:19:22.739 --> 00:19:23.630
And obviously, these individuals

00:19:23.630 --> 00:19:25.430
receive none of the kinds of protections

00:19:25.430 --> 00:19:27.753
when people accuse them of misconduct.

00:19:28.628 --> 00:19:30.756
And one of the more meta things that

00:19:30.756 --> 00:19:32.488
definitely strikes close to home

00:19:32.488 --> 00:19:35.000
is that we have a lot
of laws in this country.

00:19:35.000 --> 00:19:37.970
And theoretically, every single law

00:19:37.970 --> 00:19:40.340
that's on the books could result

00:19:40.340 --> 00:19:43.410
in someone with a gun
trying to enforce it.

00:19:43.410 --> 00:19:46.500
And we've seen relatively
minor infractions

00:19:46.500 --> 00:19:49.506
leading to a suspect being killed.

00:19:49.506 --> 00:19:50.957
Whether it's kiting checks,

00:19:50.957 --> 00:19:53.360
or passing a bad bill, or trying to sell

00:19:53.360 --> 00:19:55.720
cigarettes without the
correct certification.

00:19:55.720 --> 00:19:57.380
Those are all examples of people

00:19:58.330 --> 00:20:00.650
who were accused of minor, minor crimes

00:20:00.650 --> 00:20:02.450
that because the police were called,

00:20:02.450 --> 00:20:06.720
and admittedly, they
had breached those laws,

00:20:06.720 --> 00:20:09.050
they were detained using violent force

00:20:09.050 --> 00:20:12.173
and individuals died as a result of those.

00:20:13.080 --> 00:20:14.270
So, perhaps we need to rethink

00:20:14.270 --> 00:20:16.934
some of the laws that are on the books,

00:20:16.934 --> 00:20:18.450
or at the very least, authorization

00:20:18.450 --> 00:20:21.450
of using physical or violent force

00:20:21.450 --> 00:20:26.370
to detain people who are
accused of very minor crimes.

00:20:26.370 --> 00:20:28.616
Now, some people might not like that

00:20:28.616 --> 00:20:31.120
because it might lead to
laws being not enforced.

00:20:31.120 --> 00:20:33.940
But I think as a society
we need to ask ourselves

00:20:33.940 --> 00:20:36.950
what kind of laws justify the potential

00:20:36.950 --> 00:20:40.250
for someone dying as a
result of breaching that law.

00:20:40.250 --> 00:20:42.420
And since we're on the topic of

00:20:42.420 --> 00:20:44.330
police enforcing minor,
sometimes misdemeanor laws,

00:20:44.330 --> 00:20:46.740
perhaps we might consider dissociating

00:20:46.740 --> 00:20:48.782
the revenue that comes from

00:20:48.782 --> 00:20:50.280
those penalties from police departments.

00:20:50.280 --> 00:20:51.830
Because often the police departments

00:20:51.830 --> 00:20:54.130
directly benefit from that revenue.

00:20:54.130 --> 00:20:57.118
It's hard to not think of Ferguson

00:20:57.118 --> 00:21:00.003
where there was an entire police culture

00:21:00.003 --> 00:21:01.140
of trying to get as much money

00:21:01.140 --> 00:21:03.364
from penalties as possible.

00:21:03.364 --> 00:21:04.809
Things like speeding tickets

00:21:04.809 --> 00:21:05.642
or penalties from misdemeanors.

00:21:05.642 --> 00:21:07.950
All of that revenue,
when it was generated,

00:21:07.950 --> 00:21:10.010
often from very low income communities,

00:21:10.010 --> 00:21:12.507
went directly to the police department.

00:21:12.507 --> 00:21:13.930
So, it created this terrible incentive

00:21:13.930 --> 00:21:16.160
of being very, very particular about

00:21:16.160 --> 00:21:17.620
enforcing everything and getting

00:21:17.620 --> 00:21:19.760
as much money as possible
from the community.

00:21:19.760 --> 00:21:21.440
So, if the police department didn't

00:21:21.440 --> 00:21:23.571
benefit from that revenue,

00:21:23.571 --> 00:21:25.336
it might create an incentive

00:21:25.336 --> 00:21:26.938
not necessarily to enforce some of

00:21:26.938 --> 00:21:28.080
these very low level infractions.

00:21:28.080 --> 00:21:29.330
And speaking of the community,

00:21:29.330 --> 00:21:33.059
we might want more community
involvement from police.

00:21:33.059 --> 00:21:34.220
Some of the police departments that

00:21:34.220 --> 00:21:36.810
have done a generally very good job

00:21:36.810 --> 00:21:39.636
have been proactive about trying to

00:21:39.636 --> 00:21:40.980
make sure that not only the
high level police officers,

00:21:40.980 --> 00:21:43.240
but even mid and low level police officers

00:21:43.240 --> 00:21:44.560
are involved in the community.

00:21:44.560 --> 00:21:47.040
People often talk about requiring police

00:21:47.972 --> 00:21:50.140
to live in the areas that
they actively police.

00:21:50.140 --> 00:21:51.990
But that's probably more of a proxy

00:21:51.990 --> 00:21:53.850
for just knowing the community itself,

00:21:53.850 --> 00:21:56.880
knowing who is there,
getting to know those people.

00:21:56.880 --> 00:21:59.490
And instead of all police interactions

00:21:59.490 --> 00:22:03.361
being only when things have gone wrong,

00:22:03.361 --> 00:22:04.806
showing that the police presence

00:22:04.806 --> 00:22:06.246
is there to help the community,

00:22:06.246 --> 00:22:08.173
to listen to the community, ahead of time,

00:22:08.173 --> 00:22:10.270
and really having that
institutional knowledge

00:22:10.270 --> 00:22:12.823
instead of waiting for things to go bad

00:22:12.823 --> 00:22:13.940
and only having police interactions

00:22:13.940 --> 00:22:15.590
being at the point of a gun.

00:22:15.590 --> 00:22:17.890
Though one of the issues here is that

00:22:17.890 --> 00:22:19.930
often police departments are just

00:22:19.930 --> 00:22:22.810
a patchwork quilt throughout
the entire United States

00:22:22.810 --> 00:22:26.920
with no overarching guidelines
and no data sharing.

00:22:26.920 --> 00:22:28.640
And often the police don't have

00:22:28.640 --> 00:22:29.980
the budget to do those sort of things.

00:22:29.980 --> 00:22:31.270
So, it might be important for the federal

00:22:31.270 --> 00:22:33.921
government to take an oversight role here.

00:22:33.921 --> 00:22:35.240
Perhaps there can be a central federal

00:22:35.240 --> 00:22:38.565
oversight or data clearinghouse center

00:22:38.565 --> 00:22:40.649
where the federal government provides

00:22:40.649 --> 00:22:41.580
the funds and the manpower to

00:22:41.580 --> 00:22:43.680
gather all of the relevant statistics

00:22:43.680 --> 00:22:45.910
and to provide some oversight to

00:22:45.910 --> 00:22:47.260
all of the different states

00:22:48.184 --> 00:22:50.274
and individual police
departments throughout.

00:22:50.274 --> 00:22:51.330
And, on a more coercive tact,

00:22:51.330 --> 00:22:52.280
perhaps the federal government

00:22:52.280 --> 00:22:53.330
through the Department of Justice

00:22:53.330 --> 00:22:54.450
needs to get more involved

00:22:54.450 --> 00:22:57.150
in terms of investigating
individual police departments

00:22:57.150 --> 00:22:59.950
in states that have a systemic issue.

00:22:59.950 --> 00:23:01.450
Effectively over the last few years

00:23:01.450 --> 00:23:04.260
the department has stopped investigating

00:23:04.260 --> 00:23:05.770
individual police departments.

00:23:05.770 --> 00:23:08.562
I can't remember the time
the Department of Justice

00:23:08.562 --> 00:23:10.120
issued a consent decree against

00:23:10.120 --> 00:23:11.840
individual police departments

00:23:11.840 --> 00:23:14.637
to help enforce rights.

00:23:14.637 --> 00:23:15.670
And that's a worrying trend,

00:23:15.670 --> 00:23:18.323
because that's one of the oversight roles

00:23:18.323 --> 00:23:19.769
of the Department of Justice.

00:23:19.769 --> 00:23:21.524
And while the federal government should

00:23:21.524 --> 00:23:23.455
probably have some oversight role

00:23:23.455 --> 00:23:24.430
